{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim is true: many white people have resisted oppressive governments. Examples include Germans who opposed the Nazi regime, such as the White Rose movement; Polish Solidarity activists who challenged communist rule; and white South Africans who fought apartheid.\n\nBut “white people” is a broad racial category, not a unified political group. These examples establish that some white people resisted oppression; they do not imply that all did, or that resistance is inherent to any race. Others supported those same governments. Resistance is better understood through people’s actions, values, and circumstances than their race."}}
